      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "PART A: BACKGROUND OF ECONOMIC REFORMS IN INDIA"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Economic Crisis of 1991 forced India to introduce the New Economic Policy (NEP) to restore macroeconomic stability.",
        "Liberalisation focused on removing government quotas, licenses, and trade restrictions to deregulate Indian industries.",
        "Privatisation involved transferring ownership and management of public sector enterprises (PSUs) to private entities.",
        "Globalisation aimed at integrating the Indian economy with the world economy through trade, capital, and technology flows.",
        "LPG reforms resulted in higher GDP growth, increased Foreign Direct Investment (FDI), and expanded foreign exchange reserves."
      ],
      "id": "8qj70rf"
